Chamfers and fillets: a contact along a chain of edges of the face the
blend consumes (mitcad#334).

ChFi3d_ContactsAlongFaceEdges puts a contact that runs along an edge E of
its face F (a strip as wide as the chamfer or the rounding, consumed) onto
the face beyond E, along a whole edge (0140) or along a part of it (0148).
Two cases were left:

- A contact along several edges of F end to end (the strip's far side split
  at a vertex where nothing else meets it, or where another face does) was
  along none of them: F kept a strip of no width along them, a face of zero
  area with open wires. Such a contact's SurfData is now split at the
  chain's inner vertices (ChFi3d_SplitAtVertexOnLine, as for a closed edge's
  vertex, 0144), each part along one edge; the chain's first and last edges
  may go on past the contact's ends.
- A contact along a part of an edge (the chamfered chain ending inside the
  strip's far side, the strip going on past the chain's end) whose other
  end is at a vertex of E kept a point of its own there, while the next
  SurfData's contact along the next edge of F ended at that vertex: the
  edges of the face beyond took two vertices at one place and its wires were
  not closed. That end is now the vertex too, as for a contact along a whole
  edge.

A boss on a slab 2 wider all round, the boss's foot chamfered (or rounded)
by 2 along three sides and the corner arcs: the slab's top vanishes along
them and stays along the fourth side. Before, the result was invalid (and
with the far side split, one face too many); now it is valid with the exact
volume.

Upstream: new.
